  "description": "The falling zigzag, run into its own last external low. One candle wicks three below the level and closes above it; the next closes one below on a shallower low. Only the second is a break.",
  "labelling_rationale": "The mirror of s4-close-through-breaks-structure-a-wick-does-not, on the falling zigzag. \u00a73.5 states the bearish case as 'mirror for bearish BOS below the external swing low', and a rule stated by reflection still needs a case: the one thing this catches that its twin cannot is a detector that hard-codes the bullish comparison.\n\nThe zigzag is unchanged through absolute index 299. Its last external swing is the peak at 294, which confirms five candles later at 299 -- so 299 is the first candle on which the trend is BEARISH and the first on which a bearish BOS can be evaluated. The most recent confirmed external swing low at that point is the trough at 288, whose low is 11, and it is unconsumed.\n\nTwo candles are added, and they are the whole case.\n\nIndex 300 puts its low at 8, three below the level, and closes at 12 -- above it. \u00a73.5's wick-only rule is symmetric: a penetration whose close returns inside the level is not a break, it is a sweep candidate for the Liquidity Engine.\n\nIndex 301 closes at 10, one below the level, on a candle whose low of 9 is *higher* than the previous candle's. As in the bullish twin, the inversion is deliberate and is the point: a detector keyed on how far price travelled would break at 300 and not at 301. \u00a73.5 reads the close.\n\nThe replay uses epsilon = 0, so the comparison is Cl < 11 exactly: 12 fails, 10 passes. The break records against swing 288 at break_index 301, and the swing is marked consumed_by BOS_DOWN.",
  "labelled_by": "drafted by Claude Opus 5 2026-08-23 from SLS 3.4/3.5; VERIFIED by developer 2026-08-23 against a rendered view of the series -- confirmed the shape and the doctrine question the case turns on (a wick through the level with the close back inside is not a break). Per-candle arithmetic rests on the generator's assertions, as with its three siblings.",
